<Project>
  <!--
    .cab MUST NOT BE SIGNED, AND THIS IS A TWO-LINE FIX RATHER THAN ONE.

    Arcade 11 added .cab to its DEFAULT FileExtensionSignInfo:

        Sign.props:86   <FileExtensionSignInfo Include=".dll;.exe;.mibc;.msi;.cab"
                                               CertificateName="Microsoft400" />

    Arcade 9 defined no .cab at all. This branch carries one - the 2.8 MB
    IntelliTraceCollection.cab that Microsoft.TestPlatform ships under
    tools\net451\Common7\IDE\CommonExtensions\Microsoft\IntelliTrace - and it arrives
    ALREADY SIGNED from the pinned Microsoft.Internal.Intellitrace package. Under the new
    default it was signed AGAIN, and that is a change to shipped content on a branch whose
    mandate is to reproduce what 16.11 originally shipped.

    MEASURED on build 3065144, which delivered before this fix:

        pinned package / shipped 2021   2827942 bytes   cert serial 33000001DF...01DF
                                                        notBefore 2020-12-15
        3065144 delivered               2828878 bytes   cert serial 330000021D...021D
                                                        notBefore 2026-04-16

    A 2026 certificate cannot be the 2021 signature. The +936 bytes are the new one.

    WHY THE Remove IS REQUIRED. Arcade 11 rejects a duplicate definition, so simply adding
    .cab -> None is a hard build error. Remove first, then re-add.

    AND DO NOT "FIX" A COLLISION BY DELETING THE COLLIDING LINE. That builds green and
    silently starts signing the cab again, which is the defect this exists to prevent. The
    17.14 line hit the same error and preserved its entry this way; their delivered cab is
    byte-identical to their pinned package under Arcade 11.

    THIS IS THE ONLY AFFECTED EXTENSION HERE, measured rather than assumed: of the eight
    Arcade 11 added (.cab .msi .deb .rpm .pkg .app .dylib .tar.gz), a scan INSIDE the
    produced packages finds 4 .cab entries and zero of the rest. Note "inside" - a
    filesystem search for *.cab across the build output returns 0, because the file only
    exists within the nupkgs.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <FileExtensionSignInfo Remove=".cab" />
    <FileExtensionSignInfo Include=".cab" CertificateName="None" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    These are third party libraries that we use in Arcade. We need to sign them even if they
    are already signed. However, they must be signed with a 3rd party certificate.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <FileSignInfo Include="AWSSDK.Core.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="AWSSDK.S3.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="CommandLine.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="Handlebars.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="SleetLib.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="Swashbuckle.AspNetCore.Swagger.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="Octokit.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="YamlDotNet.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="Mono.Options.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="Newtonsoft.Json.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="Newtonsoft.Json.Bson.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="SevenZip.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="winterop.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="wix.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="LibGit2Sharp.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="SharpYaml.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />

    <!-- Despite being called "Microsoft.*", these are not produced by Microsoft. These assemblies come from the Wix toolset project. -->
    <FileSignInfo Include="Microsoft.Deployment.Compression.Cab.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="Microsoft.Deployment.Compression.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="Microsoft.Deployment.Resources.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="Microsoft.Deployment.WindowsInstaller.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
    <FileSignInfo Include="Microsoft.Deployment.WindowsInstaller.Package.dll" CertificateName="3PartySHA2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ==========================================================================================
    WHERE THIS LINE'S ARTIFACTS ACTUALLY ARE

    Arcade's default ItemsToSign globs are $(ArtifactsPackagesDir)**\*.nupkg and
    $(VisualStudioSetupOutputPath)**\*.vsix, which resolve to

        artifacts\packages\Release\{Shipping,NonShipping}\*.nupkg
        artifacts\VSSetup\Release\**\*.vsix

    On this line the packages are not produced by Arcade. They are produced by
    scripts\build.ps1, which packs nuspec files with nuget.exe into

        artifacts\Release\packages          $TPB_PackageOutDir,  scripts\build.ps1:153
        artifacts\Release\VSIX              $vsixProjectDir,     Create-VsixPackage

    The two sets of paths do not intersect, so without the entries below the signing step
    resolves an empty ItemsToSign. Arcade's AllowEmptySignList is false for in-build signing,
    so that is an error rather than a silent no-op - but it is still a failure, and this is
    what avoids it.

    Adding globs here rather than moving the script's output is deliberate. $TPB_PackageOutDir
    and the artifacts\Release layout are read by a dozen-plus copy sites in scripts\build.ps1;
    changing the root re-opens every one of them. This file is Arcade's extension point and no
    artifact comparison reads it. Copying the packages into Arcade's layout was also considered
    and rejected: it would leave two copies of every package on disk and two answers to "which
    one is the artifact".

    The globs are recursive because Create-VsixPackage builds a project into that tree and the
    .vsix lands under it rather than directly in it. Globbing wide and then ASSERTING THE SET is
    the safe direction; globbing narrowly and hoping is not.
    ==========================================================================================
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <_ServicingPackageOutDir>$([MSBuild]::NormalizeDirectory('$(ArtifactsDir)', '$(Configuration)', 'packages'))</_ServicingPackageOutDir>
    <_ServicingVsixOutDir>$([MSBuild]::NormalizeDirectory('$(ArtifactsDir)', '$(Configuration)', 'VSIX'))</_ServicingVsixOutDir>
    <_ServicingProducedPackagesFile>$(ArtifactsDir)$(Configuration)\produced-packages.txt</_ServicingProducedPackagesFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ItemsToSign Include="$(_ServicingPackageOutDir)**\*.nupkg" />
    <ItemsToSign Include="$(_ServicingVsixOutDir)**\*.vsix" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    A glob that matches NOTHING fails loudly, because Arcade already checks for it. A glob that
    matches SOME of the packages signs some of the packages and exits 0, and nothing downstream
    can tell that apart from success. That is the case this target exists for.

    Create-NugetPackages writes produced-packages.txt AFTER Assert-PackagesProduced has
    established that the output directory holds exactly the set of packages the nuspec files
    asked for, so the file is the produced SET and not merely a listing of whatever is on disk.
    This target joins that set against the resolved ItemsToSign BY NAME and reports the two
    directions separately. They are never netted: one missing and one unexpected is two findings,
    not zero.

    A file that does not exist is an error, not a skip. "I could not look" must not be reportable
    as "I found nothing".
  -->
  <Target Name="AssertServicingSigningSetMatchesProducedPackages" BeforeTargets="Execute">
    <Error Condition="!Exists('$(_ServicingProducedPackagesFile)')"
           Text="Cannot verify the signing set: '$(_ServicingProducedPackagesFile)' does not exist. Create-NugetPackages writes it. Signing must not run before packaging." />

    <ReadLinesFromFile File="$(_ServicingProducedPackagesFile)">
      <Output TaskParameter="Lines" ItemName="_ExpectedSignPackage" />
    </ReadLinesFromFile>

    <ItemGroup>
      <_SignNupkg Include="@(ItemsToSign)" Condition="'%(Extension)' == '.nupkg'" />
      <_SignNonPackage Include="@(ItemsToSign)" Exclude="@(_SignNupkg)" />
      <_ActualSignPackage Include="@(_SignNupkg->'%(Filename)%(Extension)')" />
      <_SignPackageMissing Include="@(_ExpectedSignPackage)" Exclude="@(_ActualSignPackage)" />
      <_SignPackageUnexpected Include="@(_ActualSignPackage)" Exclude="@(_ExpectedSignPackage)" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <Error Condition="'@(_ExpectedSignPackage)' == ''"
           Text="'$(_ServicingProducedPackagesFile)' is empty. An empty expected set would make any signing set pass." />
    <Error Condition="'@(_SignPackageMissing)' != ''"
           Text="Packages were produced but will NOT be signed: @(_SignPackageMissing, ', '). Searched $(_ServicingPackageOutDir)**\*.nupkg." />
    <Error Condition="'@(_SignPackageUnexpected)' != ''"
           Text="ItemsToSign contains packages that Create-NugetPackages did not produce: @(_SignPackageUnexpected, ', '). A stale package from an earlier run would be signed and shipped." />

    <Message Importance="high" Text="Signing set verified against the produced set, by name: @(_ActualSignPackage, ', ')" />
    <Message Importance="high" Condition="'@(_SignNonPackage)' != ''"
             Text="Also to sign, and not packages: @(_SignNonPackage->'%(Filename)%(Extension)', ', ')" />
    <Message Importance="high" Condition="'@(_SignNonPackage)' == ''"
             Text="Nothing to sign besides the packages. On a machine with no Visual Studio that is the expected absence: Create-VsixPackage stops before building the VSIX, so '$(_ServicingVsixOutDir)' holds no .vsix. On a build agent this line means the VSIX was not produced and is a finding." />
  </Target>

</Project>
